INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(4352,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7165200','Musculoskeletal Radiology Reports: Overlooked or Valuable?','Sahraian S, Alvin MD, Haj-Mirzaian A, Jalilianhasanpour R, Beheshtian E, Honig E, Shahriari M, Uzner M, Demehri S, Yousem DM.','2020','carestream stamps orthopedists orthopedists subspecialized carestream retrievable orthopedists orthopedists orthopedists orthopedists orthopedists referrers alvin referrers insurer orthopedists orthopedists orthopedists alvin orthopedists orthopedists orthopedists orthopedists referrer viewership galinato viewership orthopedists buabbas shamali haidar shawaf medinform schreyer burshteyn belden bosmans jml weyler schepper parizel rover bosmans jml schrans avonts maeseneer jbr btr jbr btr alvin shahriari honig yousem galinato alvin yousem mahesh oguz yousem deluca herskovits s1076','Abstract The electronic medical record (EMR) can reveal preferences of clinicians regarding imaging services. We sought to evaluate viewing habits for reports and images of musculoskeletal (MSK) studies by ordering clinicians. We hypothesized that MSK reports are important to clinical management, especially for advanced imaging modalities. We tracked the image and report access of all MSK studies ordered in September 2016 over 8 months using logs of the EMR (Epic Systems, Verona, WI), and by an independent analysis of the institutional PACS (Picture Archiving and Communication Systems) (Carestream Health, Rochester, NY). The time stamps were extracted for when images and reports were viewed. We categorized MSK studies by modality and provider department. We also compared the rates of viewing reports and images among different modalities and departments using the chi-square test. Of the 8143 viewed MSK studies, 7842 (96.3%) reports (with/without images) and 3916 (48.1%) imaging data (with/without reports) were viewed. Viewing reports alone occurred in 4227 (51.9%) studies. CT and MRI reports alone views occurred more often in comparison to radiographs ([482/706; 68.3%] for CT and [981/1713; 57.3%] for MRI vs. [2764/5724; 48.3%] for radiography, p < 0.001). Orthopedists ordered the highest number of MSK studies and viewed reports 99.2% (3216/3242) of the time, including a 54.6% (1770/3242) rate of viewing reports alone and a 44.6% (1446/3242) rate of viewing both reports and images. They viewed images without reports in 0.8% (27/3242) of cases. MSK reports were viewed significantly more frequently than the images across all modalities and all relevant specialties. Keywords: Musculoskeletal, Value, Viewing report, Viewing imaging, PACS, EMR Introduction The contribution of electronic health information exchange to the value-based healthcare economy has grown in the past decade. Adopting Picture Archiving and Communication Systems (PACS) as a way to display, store, and transfer medical images has positively impacted the workflow of both radiologists and clinicians [ 1 ]. Being able to immediately retrieve radiology reports using the electronic medical record (EMR) has reduced the costs and consequences associated with delays in accessing imaging findings [ 2 ]. The radiology report is the primary means of communication between radiologists and clinicians; however, some clinicians prioritize medical images over radiology reports . Previous studies suggested that most internists (> 80%) accept the important role of a radiologist’s report in practice; few believe that they are able to interpret imaging studies better than radiologists (0.4%) [ 3 ]. According to a survey in the literature, if primary care physicians attempt to interpret images themselves, clinically significant findings are missed in 84% of abnormal cases; hence, not reviewing the radiology reports seems ill-advised. Although more than half of specialists consider radiologists as superior to themselves in image interpretation [ 4 ], this impression may vary by specialty and by how comfortable the providers are with the anatomy and advanced imaging modalities (e.g., computed tomography [CT] and magnetic resonance imaging [MRI]). For instance, it has been shown that orthopedic oncologists mainly rely on radiologists’ reports to discover neoplasms on MRIs, while orthopedic surgeons are more comfortable with assessing extremity fractures by reviewing the bone radiograph images (absent reports) themselves [ 5 ]. We sought to determine the viewing habits for images and reports of MSK studies. We chose MSK studies because (1) a wide variety of modalities are used, (2) a wide range of specialists order MSK-related studies, including multiple subspecialists in orthopedics, (3) MSK studies cover a broad spectrum of pathologies including congenital, developmental, infectious, traumatic, neoplastic, and vascular diseases, and (4) we could compare with a previously published study on neuroradiological examinations [ 6 ]. We hypothesized that views of MSK reports will exceed views of images, especially for advanced imaging modalities, highlighting the importance of radiology reports. Materials and Methods This study was performed with institutional review board approval and was HIPAA compliant. Data were retrospectively gathered on all MSK imaging studies based on Current Procedural Terminology codes, from all patient settings, ordered over a 1-month period from September 1, 2016, to September 30, 2016. Study modalities included MRIs with and without intravenous contrast, CTs with and without intravenous contrast, and radiographs of the extremities, spine, and pelvis. For CT/MRI, we only included extremity and spine, as all pelvis CT/MRI imaging is interpreted by the department’s body division. Furthermore, in our institution, spine studies are separated based on the provider (e.g., spine studies ordered by orthopedists are read by MSK radiologists, whereas those ordered by neurosurgeons are read by neuroradiologists). All studies were interpreted by fellowship-trained MSK radiologists (5 attending physicians). There is attending coverage in the radiology department of our institution from 7 AM to 11 PM each day. From 11 PM to 7 AM, radiology residents put in preliminary reports that are accessible in the EMR by all physicians, but then checked by sub-specialty radiologists in the morning. The orthopedics department at our quaternary care academic medical center is comprised of 40 surgeons, most of whom are subspecialized, as discussed below. Our predefined target turnaround time (TAT) for all radiology reports in MSK department is 85% within 24 h. In addition, a maximum of 5% of reports are allowed to be signed by the attending radiologist with more than a 72-h delay. Imaging studies were reviewed for (1) when the study order was placed, (2) when the patient underwent the imaging study, (3) when the images were viewed (the first time), and (4) when the radiology reports were viewed (the first time). We traced only the first view of a study and did not count multiple views. We tracked the imaging and report access for 8 months (median of 197 days; interquartile range of 187–205 days) after the order was submitted to allow for delays in performing imaging, interpreting the imaging, and reviewing the imaging and reports. This information was obtained by reviewing logs of the EMR (Epic Systems, Verona, WI), and by an independent analysis of the institutional PACS (Carestream Health, Rochester, NY). These data are required by law to be retrievable as part of HIPAA regulations. Providers comprised faculty, trainee (medical student, resident, and fellow), and physician assistant (PA) or nurse practitioner (NP). Trainees, PAs, and NPs were classified according to their assigned departments. Our institutional credentialing office provided a list of providers with departments. We categorized MSK imaging studies by modality type into three categories: radiographs, CTs, and MRIs. We classified the study providers into 13 different departments: orthopedics, neurosurgery, emergency department (ED), pediatrics (Peds), internal medicine, surgery, obstetrics and gynecology (OB-GYN), anesthesiology, neurology, urology, otolaryngology, physical medicine and rehabilitation (PM&R), and psychiatry. We did not include radiologist views. Since a large percentage of studies were ordered by orthopedics, we also classified the departments into two categories—(1) orthopedics and (2) all other departments—for statistical analysis. We also classified the study providers based on the patient setting into four different groups: emergency, inpatient, outpatient, and community. We estimate that 95% of outpatient providers have access to EMR and PACS. To drill down on the orthopedists’ viewing habits, we performed a sub-analysis for this department based on the academic level of providers. Furthermore, we categorized faculty members of the orthopedics department by their sub-specialty into 5 divisions: general (who do not have sub-specialty clinic in our institution), extremity (surgeons with the following sub-specialty training for foot and ankle, hand, hip and knee, shoulder and elbow, sports medicine, and trauma), spine, oncology, and pediatric orthopedics. We sought to determine if those specialists relying on more advanced modalities (e.g., oncologists) varied from those relying more on radiographs (e.g., trauma). For each imaging study, we calculated the rate of viewing images, reports, both images and reports, or neither by providers based on types and departments. Using the chi-square test, we compared the percentage of viewing reports alone between different modalities and departments in order to assess the value of reports. We did the same analysis for viewing images alone . Because of numerous potential influencing factors [ 7 ], we discarded any cases in which neither the report nor the images were viewed for the purposes of these analyses (3215/11358; 28.3%). Finally, age, gender, and ethnicity of all patients were acquired from de-identified patient data. All analyses were done using SPSS (v.24, Chicago, IL). Statistical significance was defined as p < 0.05. Results Demographics There were 11,358 imaging studies specific to MSK radiology ordered in September 2016. The studies were ordered for 6626 unique patients. The mean age of patients was 46.5 years with 44.7% male patients and 58% Caucasians. Of these, viewing data from PACS, RIS (radiological information system) and the EMR were available for 8143 studies. For the remaining 3215 (28.3%), images and reports were never viewed at all (neither view). XR studies were most likely to have neither the images nor the reports seen (2663/8387; 31.8%) when compared with MRIs (402; 2115 19.0%) or CTs (150/856; 17.5%) ( p < 0.001). From this point onward, we only considered the 8143 studies with image and report viewing information. Results by Modality The distribution of studies by type appears in Fig. 1 and Table 1 . Radiographs were the most commonly ordered studies (5724/8143; 70.3%) followed by MRIs (1713/8143; 21.0%) and CTs (706/8143; 8.7%). Fig. 1. Open in a new tab MSK imaging and report views by modality. Overall rate of viewing images alone , reports alone , and both for the three imaging modality categories. MSK, musculoskeletal; XR, radiograph; MRI, magnetic resonance imaging; CT, computed tomography Table 1. MSK studies based on modality Modality Departments Imaging only Reports only Both viewed Total XR Orthopedics 24 1460 1170 2654 0.9% 55.0% 44.1% Others 259 1304 1507 3070 8.4% 42.5% 49.1% All 283 2764 2677 5724 4.9% 48.3% 46.8% MRI Orthopedics 3 243 229 475 0.6% 51.1% 48.2% Others 9 738 491 1238 0.7% 59.6% 39.7% All 12 981 720 1713 0.7% 57.3% 42.0% CT Orthopedics 0 69 45 114 0.0% 60.5% 39.5% Others 6 413 173 592 1.0% 69.8% 29.2% All 6 482 218 706 0.8% 68.3% 30.9% Total Orthopedics 27 1772 1444 3243 0.8% 54.6% 44.5% Others 274 2455 2171 4900 5.6% 50.1% 44.3% All 301 4227 3615 8143 3.7% 51.9% 44.4% Open in a new tab Data are presented as number (% total) Others included Neurosurgery, Emergency Department (ED), Pediatrics (Peds), Internal Medicine, Surgery, Obstetrics and Gynecology (OB-GYN), Anesthesiology, Neurology, Urology, Otolaryngology, Physical medicine and rehabilitation (PM&R), and Psychiatry departments MSK , musculoskeletal; XR , radiograph; MRI , magnetic resonance imaging; CT , computed tomography Overall, 96.3% (7842/8143) of reports (with/without images) were viewed, while images (with/without reports) were viewed in 44.4% (3916/8143) of studies. Viewing reports alone occurred in 51.9% (4227/8143) of all studies. CT and MRI viewing reports alone occurred more often ([482/706; 68.3%] and [981/1713; 57.3%], respectively) than for radiographs (2764/5724; 48.3%) ( p < 0.001). However, viewing images alone occurred in just 3.7% (301/8143) of all studies, with radiographs (283/5724; 4.9%) higher in comparison to both CT (6/706; 0.8%) and MRI (12/1713; 0.7%) ( p < 0.001). Table 1 also demonstrates the distribution of MSK studies ordered by the orthopedics department in comparison to all other departments based on different modalities. In both groups, radiographs were the most commonly ordered studies, followed by CTs and MRIs. Regarding radiographs, the rate at which the orthopedists viewed reports alone was (1460/2654; 55.0%), compared with all other departments (1304/3070; 42.5%). The difference of viewing reports alone between orthopedists and all other departments (55.0% versus 42.5%) was statistically significant ( p < 0.001). However, all other departments viewed the reports of CTs and MRIs (413/592; 69.8%, and 738/1238; 59.6%, respectively) more than orthopedists ([69/114; 60.5%] and [243/475; 51.1%], respectively) (CTs p = 0.05, MRI p < 0.001). Results by Specialty The frequencies of orders, viewing images, and viewing reports of all studies among all departments appear in Table 2 . Orthopedics (3243/8143; 39.8%) ordered the most studies, followed by internal medicine (1531/8143; 18.8%) and EM (1354/8143; 16.6%). Table 2. MSK studies by departments Department Imaging only Reports only Both viewed Total Total 301 4227 3615 8143 3.70% 51.9% 44.4% Orthopedics 27 1772 1444 3243 0.8% 54.6% 44.5% Other 274 2455 2171 4900 5.6% 50.1% 44.3% Anesthesiology 0 22 24 46 0% 47.8% 52.2% EM 114 665 575 1354 8.4% 49.1% 42.5% Internal Medicine 40 840 651 1531 2.6% 54.9% 42.5% Neurology 5 177 58 240 2.1% 73.8% 24.2% Neurosurgery 21 354 336 711 3.0% 49.8% 47.3% OB-GYN 0 10 8 18 0% 55.6% 44.4% Otolaryngology 0 3 3 6 0% 50% 50% Pediatrics 85 207 369 661 12.9% 31.3% 55.8% PM&R 0 21 32 53 0% 39.6% 60.4% Psychiatry 2 2 15 19 10.5% 10.5% 78.9% Surgery 5 128 99 232 2.2% 55.2% 42.7% Urology 0 10 1 11 0% 90.9% 9.1% Open in a new tab Data are presented as number (% total) Others included Neurosurgery, Emergency Department (ED), Pediatrics (Peds), Internal Medicine, Surgery, Obstetrics and Gynecology (OB-GYN), Anesthesiology, Neurology, Urology, Otolaryngology, Physical medicine and rehabilitation (PM&R), and Psychiatry departments MSK , musculoskeletal; EM , emergency medicine; OB-GYN , obstetrics and gynecology; PM&R , physical medicine and rehabilitation Of the departments that ordered more than 50 MSK studies, neurology (240/8143 studies ordered) had the highest rate of viewing reports alone (177/240; 73.8%) while pediatrics (661/8143 studies ordered) had the lowest rate (207/661; 31.3%) (Table 2 ). Pediatrics also had the highest rate of viewing images alone (85/661; 12.9%) while orthopedic surgery (27/3243; 0.8%) had the lowest rate. The rate of viewing images alone by non-orthopedic departments was significantly higher (274/4900; 5.6%) than orthopedists (27/3243; 0.8%) ( p < 0.001). Table 2 displays the frequency of total orders, viewing images, and viewing reports in orthopedics compared with those in all other departments. Orthopedic Sub-analysis (Table 3 ) Table 3. MSK studies specific to orthopedics by provider type Orthopedics Imaging only Reports only Both viewed Total Total 27 1772 1444 3243 0.8% 54.6% 44.5% Trainee (total) 12 1200 829 2041 0.6% 58.8% 40.6% Faculty (total) 15 572 615 1202 1.2% 47.6% 51.2% General 1 30 18 49 2.0% 61.2% 36.7% Extremities 3 320 425 748 0.4% 42.8% 56.8% Spine 5 100 73 178 2.8% 56.2% 41.0% Oncology 1 7 7 15 6.7% 46.7% 46.7% Pediatric 5 115 92 212 2.4% 54.2% 43.4% Open in a new tab Data are presented as number (% total) The rates of viewing reports alone were significantly different among orthopedic divisions ( p < 0.001). The general division (30/49; 61.2%) had the highest rate of viewing reports alone , whereas the extremity division (320/748; 42.8%), followed by the oncology division (7/15; 46.7%), had the lowest rates (Table 3 ). The rates of viewing images alone were also significantly different among orthopedic divisions ( p < 0.001). The oncology division (1/15; 6.7%) had the highest rate (despite small sample size and lack of power) and the extremity division (3/748; 0.4%) had the lowest rate. Results by Patient Setting The frequencies of orders, viewing images, and viewing reports of all studies divided into patient setting appear in Table 4 . Most of the studies were ordered in the outpatient setting (3924/8143; 48.2%), followed by the emergency setting (2082/8143; 25.6%). The community setting had the highest rate of viewing reports alone (252/432; 58.3%), while the emergency setting had the lowest rate (929/2082; 44.6%). By contrast, those ordered in the emergency setting had the highest rate of viewing images alone (203/2082; 9.7%) ( p < 0.001), while those ordered in the community setting (1/432; 10.2%) had the lowest rate. Table 4. MSK studies by patient setting Setting Imaging only Reports only Both viewed Total Emergency 203 929 950 2082 9.7% 44.6% 45.6% Inpatient 30 869 806 1705 1.8% 51% 47.3% Outpatient 67 2177 1680 3924 1.7% 55.5% 42.8% Community 1 252 179 432 0.2% 58.3% 41.4% Open in a new tab Data are presented as number (% total) Image Views When adding rates of viewing images alone and viewing both images and reports together, one gets a metric of patterns of viewing radiology MSK images. The data show that less than half of the MRs (732/1713; 42.7%) and CTs (224/706; 31.7%) imaging studies were ever viewed, whereas 51.7% (2960/5724) of radiographic studies were viewed ( p < 0.001) (Table 1 ). In the departmental analysis (Table 2 ), anesthesiology (24/46; 52.2%), emergency medicine (689/1354; 50.9%), neurosurgery (357/711; 50.3%), otolaryngology (3/6; 50%), pediatrics (454/661; 68.7%), PM&R (32/53; 60.4%), and psychiatry (17/19; 89.4%) viewed greater than 50% of the MSK studies. Orthopedics viewed 45.3% (1471/3243). Discussion Although one would think that the radiology report plays a critical role in patient care, few studies have determined whether radiology reports are actually viewed by referrers [ 2 , 8 ]. Except for Alvin et al. [ 6 ], studies have relied on surveying physicians regarding their report reading habits, using the clinicians’ self-reports instead of information system–based raw data [ 3 ]. Using the EMR and PACS auditing tools, researchers can now ascertain objectively when and by whom an image or a report is viewed, based on logs for who opens the study or report. In the present study, we analyzed images viewed alone, reports viewed alone, and both images and reports viewed as a surrogate of viewing habits of different specialties of medicine. Our underlying assumption was that the radiologist report, if never opened, was not assisting in patient care. We wanted to address whether the referrers were placing more emphasis on the images or the reports. Value in radiology is difficult to measure and varies depending on the stakeholder (e.g., hospital, patient, insurer) and clinical scenario (e.g., acute vs. chronic disease) [ 2 ]. We found that providers viewed MSK reports alone (without viewing the accompanying images) (4227/8143; 51.9%) or in addition to the images (3615/8143; 44.4%) in the vast majority of cases (96.3% of the time). By contrast, MSK study images alone (without viewing the accompanying report) were accessed only 3.7% (301/8143) of the time (Table 1 ). These rates varied from specialty to specialty, but views of images alone without referring to reports peaked at 12.9% in pediatrics with several departments (orthopedics, anesthesiology, OB-GYN, otolaryngology, PM&R, and urology [some with under 50 studies ordered]) viewing the reports in over 99% of their cases (Table 2 ). Aligned with our hypothesis, reports (7842/8143; 96.3%) were reviewed twice as often as images (3916/8143; 48.1%), further implying that there is more reliance on the radiologist reports rather than the images. Our data showed that orthopedics had a higher preference to view reports rather than images. Specifically, orthopedists viewed images in less than 1% (27/3243) of all MSK studies (i.e., without accessing reports), while 54.6% (1772/3243) of reports were viewed without image review. This agrees with a report from the American Association of Orthopaedic Surgeons, which showed that, of those practices offering in-office imaging services, the majority have their images interpreted by a radiologist (60%), with double interpretation by orthopedists. Only in 2% of the orthopedic offices surveyed were the images interpreted by the orthopedists alone [ 9 ]. We believe that the need for efficiency in busy offices and the time constraints imposed by declining reimbursement per unit case may drive the impetus to refer to radiology reports alone rather than opening the PACS and reviewing images on MSK patients. Besides, some believe that the radiologist can interpret imaging studies more accurately because of the radiologist’s experience and competency with advanced imaging modalities. Similar in design to the present study, Alvin et al. assessed the utilization of reports and images in neuroradiology [ 6 ]. The distribution of viewing neuroradiology images and reports by neurosurgeons was in opposition to our results with orthopedics. Neurosurgeons had both a very high image view rate (72.3% vs. 45.3% for orthopedists in our study) and report view rate (89.8%). Additionally, they had a high ratio of images viewed alone to reports viewed alone. These authors proposed that non-neuroscience specialties favored reading the reports over the imaging partly because of a lower number of studies ordered and lack of comfort compared with the neuroscience specialties. We found that extremity orthopedists viewed images alone (0.4%) less frequently than other divisions of orthopedics, possibly because of greater diversity in order types and locations, and lower volumes of studies in other divisions. Interestingly, extremity orthopedists, who mainly work with radiographs, also had the lowest rate of viewing reports alone without reviewing the images. Oncology orthopedists had the highest percentage of viewing only images (6.7%). This differed from previous studies that reported orthopedic oncologists mainly rely on radiologists’ interpretations to discover neoplasms within advanced modalities [ 5 ]; however, this discrepancy could be merely due to the small sample size in our study (i.e., only 15 oncology MSK studies that month). Alternatively, it could be due to the wide participation in regular multi-disciplinary orthopedic oncology conferences in our institution, where surgeons can communicate and discuss imaging interpretations by MSK radiologists without directly reading the reports. Our results show that the rate of viewing images alone (203/2082; 9.7%) was much higher in the emergency setting compared with other patient settings. Since the placement of imaging equipment has become more widespread, the use of emergency diagnostic imaging has increased [ 10 ]. Therefore, emergency physicians and trainees have gained training, knowledge, and skills in interpreting imaging studies [ 11 , 12 ]. On the other hand, physicians in community practice usually are not required to make immediate diagnostic decisions. Hence, they may prefer to wait to read radiologist’s reports alone prior to their documented clinical assessment or treatment plan (252/432; 58.3%). Limitations The present study has multiple limitations. First, the follow-up period of 8 months after ordering the imaging study was not based on any particular criteria; nevertheless, we felt confident that this period could account for patient delays in completing the tests and providers viewing the reports at regular outpatient follow-up intervals (e.g., 3- or 6-month oncologic follow-up). Second, the algorithm for obtaining the data was placed on a referrer accessing the radiology report in the EMR system. This was a reliable, yet suboptimal, way of evaluating provider viewership of reports, but complied with HIPAA regulations. For example, critical findings discussed over the telephone or in-person at the radiology reading room may cause a provider not to view a report or imaging study. However, since our MSK radiologist group does not often have orthopedic surgeons to call or visit the reading room, we believe that this does not account for a significant source of error. Third, the study evaluated only the first viewing of each study, limiting the interpretation for greater reliance on imaging or reports based on the total number of views over time. Studies where neither the images nor the reports were viewed could not be analyzed. The reasons for these “non-views” have been previously explained by Galinato et al. [ 7 ] with suggestions for remediation. Despite these limitations, our study represents the first comprehensive and objective assessment of MSK imaging and report viewership using the EMR. Conclusions Reliance on images and reports varies by department and modality. Only 3.7% of MSK images were viewed alone without accessing reports. Even among orthopedists, referring to the MSK study reports is nearly universal. Since the institutional practice patterns vary based on several variables, future multi-institutional studies should aim at strategies by which radiologists provide added value to patient care. Compliance with Ethical Standards This study was performed with institutional review board approval and was HIPAA compliant. Conflict of Interest The authors declare that they have no conflict of interest. Footnotes IRB Approval #00102719 was obtained for this study. Publisher’s Note Springer Nature remains neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations. References 1. Buabbas AJ, Al-Shamali DA, Sharma P, Haidar S, Al-Shawaf H. Users’ perspectives on a picture archiving and communication system (PACS): An in-depth study in a teaching hospital in Kuwait. JMIR Med. Informatics. 2016;4:e21. doi: 10.2196/medinform.5703. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Dako F, Schreyer K, Burshteyn M, Cohen G, Belden C. Expanding radiology’s role in a value-based health economy. J. Am. Coll. Radiol. 2017;14:622–624. doi: 10.1016/j.jacr.2016.10.028. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Bosmans JML, Weyler JJ, De Schepper AM, Parizel PM. The radiology report as seen by radiologists and referring clinicians: Results of the COVER and ROVER surveys. Radiology. 2011;259:184–195. doi: 10.1148/radiol.10101045. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Bosmans JML, Schrans D, Avonts D, De Maeseneer JM. Communication between general practitioners and radiologists: Opinions, experience, promises, pitfalls. JBR-BTR. 2014;97:325–330. doi: 10.5334/jbr-btr.127. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. O’Connor M. Musculoskeletal imaging: What information is important to the orthopedic oncologist? Semin. Musculoskelet. Radiol. 2007;11:273–278. doi: 10.1055/s-2008-1038316. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Alvin MD, Shahriari M, Honig E, Liu L, Yousem DM. Clinical access and utilization of reports and images in neuroradiology. J. Am. Coll. Radiol. 2018;15:1723–1731. doi: 10.1016/j.jacr.2018.03.004. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Galinato A, Alvin MD, Yousem DM. Lost to follow-up: Analysis of never-viewed radiology examinations. J. Am. Coll. Radiol. 2019;16:478–481. doi: 10.1016/j.jacr.2018.08.022. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Johnson PT, Mahesh M, Fishman EK. Image wisely and choosing wisely: Importance of adult body CT protocol design for patient safety, exam quality, and diagnostic efficacy. J. Am. Coll. Radiol. 2015;12:1185–1190. doi: 10.1016/j.jacr.2015.02.021. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. MRI Survey 20004: Final Report. American Academy of Orthopaedic Surgeons. Dec 23, 2004. 10. Oguz KK, Yousem DM, Deluca T, Herskovits EH, Beauchamp NJ. Effect of emergency department CT on neuroimaging case volume and positive scan rates. Acad. Radiol. 2002;9:1018–1024. doi: 10.1016/S1076-6332(03)80477-4. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 11. Mills AM, Raja AS, Marin JR. Optimizing diagnostic imaging in the emergency department. Acad. Emerg. Med. 2015;22:625–631. doi: 10.1111/acem.12640. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 12. Accuracy of radiographic readings in the emergency department. Am. J. Emerg. Med. 2011 , 29 , 18–25. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ]',6,'2025-10-15 09:50:13','2025-10-15 09:50:13');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(4353,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6221605','Diagnostic accuracy of endoscopic ultrasound, computed tomography, magnetic resonance imaging, and endorectal ultrasonography for detecting lymph node involvement in patients with rectal cancer: A protocol for an overview of systematic reviews.','Wang X, Gao Y, Li J, Wu J, Wang B, Ma X, Tian J, Shen M, Wang J.','2018','endorectal amasar crd42018104906 endorectal hnpcc transanal mesorectal abdominoperineal endorectal erus mesorectal mesorectal erus erus reanalyzing crd42018104906 erus erus rect rect rect carcinom rect adenocarc rect rect rect sarcom probabilit probabilit overviews logitsen logitspe overviews amasar planed polished jiarui xueni jinhui minghui jiancheng jiancheng jipin jipin xueni jinhui jiancheng jinhui jiancheng erus endorectal 17gssy2 endoluminal o129 puli bechtold atkin morson cuzick rectosigmoid orsini ascherio rimm paskett rohan delbeke maffione chondrogiannis capirci kapiteijn marijnen nagtegaal mesorectal cd009867 gunderson jessup bekaii saab arnoletti dinter hofheinz hartel endorectal hydro endorectal gagliardi bayar arrayed petrillo delrio superparamagnetic petrillo granata berge dwarkasing endorectal bipat glas slors endoluminal restaging endoluminal jaspers smeulers monasta batty cattaneo mdf thombs rhe glas rutjes harbord unification ca19 e018175 macaskill irwig','Abstract Background: Rectal cancer is one of the most common tumors and is the leading cause of cancer-related deaths in developed countries. Lymph node involvement remains the strongest prognostic factor associated with a worse prognosis in patients with rectal cancer. Several systematic reviews have investigated the accuracy of endoscopic ultrasound, computed tomography, magnetic resonance imaging, and endorectal ultrasonography for lymph node involvement of rectal cancer and compared the diagnostic accuracy of different imaging techniques, but there are considerable differences in conclusions. This study aims to assess the methodological quality and reporting quality of systematic reviews and to determine which diagnostic imaging techniques is the optimal modality for the diagnosis of lymph node involvement in patients with rectal cancer. Methods: We will search PubMed, EMBASE, Cochrane Library, and Chinese Biomedicine Literature to identify relevant studies from inception to June 2018. We will include systematic reviews that evaluated the accuracy of diagnostic imaging techniques for lymph node involvement. The methodological quality will be assessed using AMASAR checklist, and the reporting quality will be assessed using PRISMA-DTA checklist. The pairwise meta-analysis and indirect comparisons will be performed using STATA V.12.0. Results: The results of this overview will be submitted to a peer-reviewed journal for publication. Conclusion: This overview will provide comprehensive evidence of different diagnostic imaging techniques for detecting lymph node involvement in patients with rectal cancer. Ethics and dissemination: Ethics approval and patient consent are not required as this study is an overview based on published systematic reviews. PROSPERO registration number: CRD42018104906. Keywords: computed tomography, diagnostic test, endorectal ultrasonography, endoscopic ultrasound, lymph node involvement, magnetic resonance imaging, overview, rectal cancer 1. Introduction Colorectal cancer is nowadays the third most common cancer in men and the second in women worldwide, accounting for 9% of new cancer cases and 9% of cancer-specific deaths. [ 1 , 2 ] Approximately 1 in 3 of these tumors are rectal cancers. [ 3 ] In the USA, rectal cancer is a major cause of mortality, and there were an estimated 39,220 new cases in 2016. [ 4 ] Risk factors for rectal cancer include familial polyposis syndromes (FAP, HNPCC), history of adenomatous polyps, diabetes mellitus, obesity, excessive alcohol, and cigarette smoking. [ 5 – 10 ] Because of the diffusion of screening programs, the incidence of this malignancy has gradually increased. [ 11 , 12 ] As in most solid malignancies, lymph node involvement remains the strongest prognostic factor associated with a worse prognosis in patients with rectal cancer. [ 13 , 14 ] However, the prognosis of rectal cancer patients depends on the disease stage at the time of diagnosis; thus, accurate disease evaluation is necessary to properly treat rectal cancer. [ 15 – 17 ] Nowadays, treatment of rectal cancer with different locations and stages mainly includes transanal endoscopic microsurgery, anterior resection with total mesorectal excision, abdominoperineal resection, and neoadjuvant chemotherapy and radiotherapy. [ 2 , 18 , 19 ] Accurate preoperative assessment of lymph node involvement is essential for selecting patients to receive optimal treatment. [ 2 , 20 ] Various modalities, endoscopic ultrasound (EUS), computed tomography (CT), magnetic resonance imaging (MRI), and endorectal ultrasonography (ERUS), for instance, have been used to assess the lymph node status. EUS is particularly effective for assessing the depth of tumor invasion into the rectal wall. [ 21 ] CT is a sensitive method for diagnosis of abdominal and pelvic diseases. It is used frequently to determine the stage of cancer and to follow progress. [ 14 ] MRI allows for comprehensive evaluation of disease stage including tumor infiltration degree, a precise assessment of the neoplasia distance by mesorectal fascia (circumferential margin) and an effective assessment of lymph nodes involvement and mesorectal infiltration. [ 22 , 23 ] ERUS is considered to be most accurate for small tumors (tumor stages 1 and 2). [ 24 ] Recently, some meta-analysis has investigated the accuracy of EUS, CT, MRI, and ERUS for lymph node involvement of rectal cancer and compared the diagnostic accuracy of different imaging techniques, [ 3 , 25 – 27 ] but there are considerable differences in conclusions. Therefore, it is of great significance to re-evaluate these systematic reviews. The objectives of this overview are to assess the methodological quality and reporting quality of systematic reviews that evaluated the diagnostic value of index tests for lymph node involvement in patients with rectal cancer and to compare the diagnostic value of different diagnostic imaging techniques for lymph node involvement by reanalyzing the results of meta-analysis. 2. Methods 2.1. Design and registration We will conduct an overview of systematic reviews of diagnostic test accuracy. The protocol is registered in PROSPERO (CRD42018104906). We will follow the Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-analysis [ 28 ] statements for reporting our overview. 2.2. Eligibility criteria 2.2.1. Type of study Systematic reviews will meet the following criteria: diagnostic imaging techniques include EUS, CT, MRI, and ERUS or combinations; evaluate the diagnostic value of index tests for lymph node involvement in patients with rectal cancer. Systematic reviews for patients with colorectal cancer will be excluded. 2.2.2. Patients We will include rectal cancer patients with lymph node involvement regardless of treatment. No limitations will be imposed on age, sex, or nationality. 2.2.3. Interventions We will regard EUS, CT, MRI, or ERUS as index tests because these tests are usually used to predict lymph node involvement in patients with rectal cancer. In addition, other index tests for detecting lymph node involvement are also included. 2.2.4. Outcomes The primary outcomes are sensitivity (SEN), specificity (SPE), positive predictive value, negative predictive value, positive likelihood ratio (PLR), negative likelihood ratio (NLR), diagnostic odds ratio (DOR), area under the curve, and their respective 95% confidence intervals (CIs). The second outcomes are methodological quality score and reporting quality score. 2.3. Data sources We will search PubMed, EMBASE, Cochrane Library, and Chinese Biomedicine Literature to identify relevant studies from inception to June 2018. Publication languages will be restricted to English and Chinese. In addition, we will check reference lists of included studies for additional references. 2.4. Search strategy We will use search terms related to rectal neoplasm, SEN, SPE, receiver operating characteristic, meta-analysis, and systematic review. Search strategy of PubMed was as follows: #1 “Rectal Neoplasms”[Mesh] #2 “rect∗ neoplasm∗”[Title/Abstract] OR “rect∗ canc∗”[Title/Abstract] OR “rect∗ carcinom∗”[Title/Abstract] OR “rect∗ adenocarc∗”[Title/Abstract] OR “rect∗ tumor∗”[Title/Abstract] OR “rect∗ tumour∗”[Title/Abstract] OR “rect∗ sarcom∗”[Title/Abstract] #3 #1 OR #2 #4 “Sensitivity AND Specificity”[Mesh] OR “False Positive Reactions”[Mesh] OR “False Negative Reactions”[Mesh] OR “ROC Curve”[Mesh] OR “Predictive Value of Tests”[Mesh] #5 sensitivity[Title/Abstract] OR specificity[Title/Abstract] OR receiver operating characteristic[Title/Abstract] OR receiver operator characteristic[Title/Abstract] OR predictive value∗[Title/Abstract] OR roc[Title/Abstract] OR pre-test odds[Title/Abstract] OR pretest odds[Title/Abstract] OR pre-test probability∗[Title/Abstract] OR pretest probability∗[Title/Abstract] OR post-test odds[Title/Abstract] OR posttest odds[Title/Abstract] OR post test probabilit∗[Title/Abstract] OR posttest probabilit∗ [Title/Abstract] OR likelihood ratio∗[Title/Abstract] OR positive predictive value∗[Title/Abstract] OR negative predictive value∗[Title/Abstract] OR false negative∗[Title/Abstract] OR false positive∗[Title Abstract] OR true negative∗ [Title/Abstract] OR true positive∗[Title/Abstract] OR fn[Title/Abstract] OR fp[Title/Abstract] OR tn[Title/Abstract] OR tp [Title/Abstract] #6 #4 OR #5 #7 “Meta-Analysis” [Publication Type] OR “Meta-Analysis as Topic”[Mesh] #8 Meta-Analysis[Title/Abstract] OR Meta-Analyses[Title/Abstract] OR Meta Analysis[Title/Abstract] OR Meta Analyses[Title/Abstract] OR gathering analysis[Title/Abstract] #9 “Network Meta-Analysis”[Mesh] #10 Network Meta-Analyses[Title/Abstract] OR Network Meta Analysis[Title/Abstract] OR Network Meta Analyses[Title/Abstract] OR Mixed Treatment Meta-Analysis[Title/Abstract] OR Mixed Treatment Meta-Analyses[Title/Abstract] OR Multiple Treatment Comparison Meta-Analysis[Title/Abstract] OR Multiple Treatment Comparison Meta Analysis[Title/Abstract] #11 Systematic evaluation[Title/Abstract] OR Systematic assessment[Title/Abstract] OR Systematic review[Title/Abstract] OR Systematic reviews[Title/Abstract] OR System evaluation[Title/Abstract] OR System Assessment[Title/Abstract] OR Systemic review[Title/Abstract] OR Systemic reviews[Title/Abstract] #12 #7 OR #8 OR #9 OR #10 OR #11 #13 #3 AND #6 AND #12 2.5. Study selection and data extraction Literature search records will be imported into ENDNOTE X7 literature management software. Two independent reviewers will screen out possibly relevant studies independently based on the title and abstract. Then, the same 2 reviewers will retrieve the full text of all possibly relevant studies to screen out the studies that meet the inclusion criteria. We will extract study characteristics from systematic reviews including the following items: author name, year of publication, country of first author, number of author, journal name, country of journal, funding, disease, number and name of index test, number and name of reference test, outcomes; methodological characteristics of systematic reviews such as types of included studies, number of included studies, samples, number and name of databases retrieved, supplemental literature search; and results of statistical analysis including SEN, SPE, likelihood ratio, predictive value, DOR, and area under curve. Disagreements will be resolved by consensus or by discussion with a third reviewer. 2.6. Quality assessment We will assess the methodological quality of included systematic reviews using the Assessment of Multiple Systematic Reviews checklist. This checklist includes 11 items with scores ranging from 0 to 11.0. Based on previous overviews, we will consider studies with a score between 0 and 4.0 to be of low quality, 5.0 and 8.0 to be of moderate quality, and 9.0 and 11.0 to be of high quality. [ 29 , 30 ] The reporting quality of included systematic reviews will be assessed according to the Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-analysis diagnostic test accuracy (PRISMA-DTA) checklist. The PRISMA-DTA statement is an expanded checklist of original PRISMA, which aims to improve the completeness and transparency of reporting of systematic reviews of diagnostic test accuracy studies. [ 31 ] This checklist consists of 27 items. The maximum score on the PRISMA-DTA is 27. The review will be considered to have major flaws if it receives a total score of ≤15.0, minor flaws if it receives a total score of 15.5 to 21.0, and minimal flaws if it receives a total score 21.5 to 27.0. [ 32 ] The quality assessment of the included systematic reviews will be performed independently by the 2 authors and the differences will be resolved through discussion to reach a consensus. 2.7. Statistical analysis 2.7.1. Pairwise meta-analysis We will perform pairwise meta-analysis with the data of pooled SEN, SPE, DOR, PLR, NLR, and their 95% CI lower limit, 95% CI upper limit using bivariate mixed-effects regression modeling with STATA V.12.0 (Stata). The between-study variance will be calculated var logitSEN and logitSPE. [ 33 , 34 ] The proportion of heterogeneity due to the threshold effect among the included studies will be calculated by the squared correlation coefficient estimated from the between-study covariance variable in the bivariate model. [ 35 ] The heterogeneity between each study will be estimated using the Q value and the inconsistency index ( I 2 ) test, and the values of 25%, 50%, and 75% for the I 2 will be indicative of low, moderate, and high statistical heterogeneity, respectively. [ 36 ] 2.7.2. Indirect comparisons between competing diagnostic tests We will calculate relative diagnostic outcomes between index tests including relative SEN, relative SPE, relative DOR, relative PLR, and relative NLR. Then, we will conduct indirect comparisons using the relative diagnostic outcomes. All analysis will be performed using Stata software (version 12.0). 2.7.3. Assessment of reporting bias If there are 10 or more studies in the network meta-analysis, we will use the funnel plot to evaluate the potential publication bias. [ 37 ] 2.7.4. Subgroup analysis Subgroup analysis will be conducted according to the difference of time period of index tests, treatment of rectal cancer, MRI field strength, types of EUS, and CT if the necessary data are available. 3. Discussion It is not uncommon to have several systematic reviews under the same topic published evaluating the same interventions, yet without consistent conclusions. [ 38 ] However, no overviews summarize evidence for systematic reviews of the diagnostic test for patients with rectal cancer. According to our knowledge, this will be the first overview to assess the methodological quality using AMASAR checklist and the reporting quality using PRISMA-DTA checklist of systematic reviews evaluating the diagnostic value of index tests for lymph node involvement in patients with rectal cancer. Moreover, we will perform indirect comparisons between different diagnostic imaging techniques, which can clearly show the differences between different modalities. We hope that the results of this overview will help clinicians and patients to select an appropriate diagnostic test for rectal cancer. Author contributions XW, YG, and JW planed and designed the research. JL, JW, and BW tested the feasibility of the study. JT, MS, and JW provided methodological advice, polished, and revised the manuscript. XW and YG wrote the manuscript. All authors approved the final version of the manuscript. Methodology: Jiarui Wu, Xueni Ma, Jinhui Tian, Minghui Shen, Jiancheng Wang. Project administration: Xin Wang, Ya Gao, Jiancheng Wang. Resources: Jipin Li, Bo Wang. Software: Jipin Li, Xueni Ma, Jinhui Tian. Supervision: Jiancheng Wang. Validation: Bo Wang. Writing – original draft: Xin Wang, Ya Gao. Writing – review and editing: Jinhui Tian, Jiancheng Wang. Footnotes Abbreviations: CI = confidence interval, CT = computed tomography, DOR = diagnostic odds ratio, ERUS = endorectal ultrasonography, EUS = Endoscopic ultrasound, MRI = magnetic resonance imaging, NLR = negative likelihood ratio, PLR = positive likelihood ratio, SEN = sensitivity, SPE = specificity. XW and YG contributed equally to this work. Funding/Support: This work is supported by the Gansu Provincial Hospital Research Fund Project (17GSSY2-7). The authors have no conflicts of interest to disclose. References [1]. Jemal A, Bray F, Center MM, et al. Global cancer statistics. CA Cancer J Clin 2011;61:69–90. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [2]. Zhang G, Cai YZ, Xu GH. Diagnostic accuracy of MRI for assessment of T category and circumferential resection margin involvement in patients with rectal cancer: a meta-analysis. Dis Colon Rectum 2016;59:789–99. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [3]. Li XT, Sun YS, Tang L, et al. Evaluating local lymph node metastasis with magnetic resonance imaging, endoluminal ultrasound and computed tomography in rectal cancer: a meta-analysis. Colorectal Dis 2015;17:O129–35. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [4]. Siegel RL, Miller KD, Jemal A. Cancer statistics, 2016. CA Cancer J Clin 2016;66:7–30. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [5]. Puli SR, Reddy JB, Bechtold ML, et al. Accuracy of endoscopic ultrasound to diagnose nodal invasion by rectal cancers: a meta-analysis and systematic review. Ann Surg Oncol 2009;16:1255–65. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [6]. Atkin WS, Morson BC, Cuzick J. Long-term risk of colorectal cancer after excision of rectosigmoid adenomas. N Engl J Med 1992;326:658–62. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [7]. Larsson SC, Orsini N, Wolk A. Diabetes mellitus and risk of colorectal cancer: a meta-analysis. J Natl Cancer Inst 2005;97:1679–87. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [8]. Cho E, Smith-Warner SA, Ritz J, et al. Alcohol intake and colorectal cancer: a pooled analysis of 8 cohort studies. Ann Intern Med 2004;140:603–13. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [9]. Giovannucci E, Ascherio A, Rimm EB, et al. Physical activity, obesity, and risk for colon cancer and adenoma in men. Ann Intern Med 1995;122:327–34. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [10]. Paskett ED, Reeves KW, Rohan TE, et al. Association between cigarette smoking and colorectal cancer in the Women''s Health Initiative. J Natl Cancer Inst 2007;99:1729–35. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [11]. Delbeke D, Martin WH. PET and PET-CT for evaluation of colorectal carcinoma. Semin Nucl Med 2004;34:209–23. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [12]. Maffione AM, Chondrogiannis S, Capirci C, et al. Early prediction of response by 18F-FDG PET/CT during preoperative therapy in locally advanced rectal cancer: a systematic review. Eur J Surg Oncol 2014;40:1186–94. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [13]. Kapiteijn E, Marijnen CA, Nagtegaal ID, et al. Preoperative radiotherapy combined with total mesorectal excision for resectable rectal cancer. N Engl J Med 2001;345:638–46. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [14]. Sun YS, Li XT, Tang L, et al. Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) versus computed tomography (CT) for the diagnosis of lymph node metastasis in preoperative rectal cancer. Cochrane Database Syst Rev 2012;5:CD009867. [ Google Scholar ] [15]. Heo SH, Kim JW, Shin SS, et al. Multimodal imaging evaluation in staging of rectal cancer. World J Gastroenterol 2014;20:4244–55. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [16]. Gunderson LL, Jessup JM, Sargent DJ, et al. Revised tumor and node categorization for rectal cancer based on surveillance,