 cowden deisenroth dionisio frithsen grulke gwinn harrill linnenbrink sams shafer setzer sobus strynar swank tornero valez wambaugh wetmore luechtefeld rowlands revamp atzeni antonellis senn grulke volarath thillainadarajah rathman wambaugh kancherla crofton grondin sciaky mcmorran wiegers wiegers mattingly d948 d954 undiscovered bondy murty usr eifrem bizon balhoff kebede fecho tropsha robokop himmelstein lizee hessler brueggeman khankhanian baranzini e26726 harland groth pettifer willighagen evelo blomberg ecker goble mons phacts biocomputing iintell shrobe szolovits steenland savitz perfluorooctanoic steenland winquist perfluorooctanoic melzer depledge henley galloway perfluorooctanoic frisbee steenland savitz ducatman perfluorooctanoic perfluorooctanesulfonate steenland winquist perfluorooctanoic liss khh finck ppars biochimie renaldi pramono sinorita purnomo asdie asdie hypoadiponectinemia indones vilar sookoian pirola gawrieh chalasani adh1b goth acatalasemia xiaoli lipogenesis burdick stelzer plaschkes twik fishilevich nudel lieder mazor dahary warshawsky golan safran genecards bioinf fishilevich olender kolker safran genic genecards baw030 dhara holler izaguirre xintaras atsdr aylward nebert arnt bhlh makishima repa lustig mangelsdorf bledsoe consler kliewer stimmel willson zavacki modica gadaleta moschetta fxr recept kerkvliet tatonetti kliewer willson pregnane sakamuru shahane attene simeonov sakamuru simeonov muegge svetnik liaw culberson feuston undiscovered ahalt kreda mandl kohane ramoni interoperable dongbohu jdromano2 comptox_ai nihms1856636 supplementary_material_for__automating_predictive_toxicology_using_comptoxai_','Abstract ComptoxAI is a new data infrastructure for computational and artificial intelligence research in predictive toxicology. Here, we describe and showcase ComptoxAI’s graph-structured knowledge base in the context of three real-world use-cases, demonstrating that it can rapidly answer complex questions about toxicology that are infeasible using previous technologies and data resources. These use-cases each demonstrate a tool for information retrieval from the knowledge base being used to solve a specific task: The “shortest path” module is used to identify mechanistic links between perfluorooctanoic acid (PFOA) exposure and nonalcoholic fatty liver disease; the “expand network” module identifies communities that are linked to dioxin toxicity; and the quantitative structure–activity relationship (QSAR) dataset generator predicts pregnane X receptor agonism in a set of 4,021 pesticide ingredients. The contents of ComptoxAI’s source data are rigorously aggregated from a diverse array of public third-party databases, and ComptoxAI is designed as a free, public, and open-source toolkit to enable diverse classes of users including biomedical researchers, public health and regulatory officials, and the general public to predict toxicology of unknowns and modes of action. Graphical Abstract INTRODUCTION A central goal of predictive toxicology is to propose and explain adverse outcomes that may result from human exposures to specific toxicants, especially when experimental and observational data on the toxicant are incomplete or unavailable. 1 Despite great interest in doing so, it has proved tremendously challenging to accurately predict high-level endpoints of toxicity, due largely to the complex and highly variable interactions that occur at different levels of biological organization following exposure to a chemical. Furthermore, the explosion of publicly available chemical data has made it impossible to rigorously assess every compound of toxicological interest through manual experimentation or literature searching alone. Artificial intelligence (AI), cheminformatics, and computational biology provide a wealth of techniques that stand ready to tackle these issues, 2 but there remains a critical need for richly structured data resources that can support these analyses. Traditionally, most databases for scientific research have been relational databases, which consist of sets of tables where rows represent entities (often a single observation) and columns represent distinct properties of those entities. 3 Current examples of relational databases designed for toxicology research include the Adverse Outcome Pathway Database (AOP-DB), 4 the ToxCast/invitroDB database, 5 and the Comparative Toxicogenomics Database. 6 In relational databases, entities are linked via foreign keys that define relationships between attributes in one table with equivalent attributes in another table. These remain the most popular way to store large amounts of scientific data, but they come with well-known disadvantages, such as their rigidity and relatively poor performance (both in terms of speed and computational resource consumption). It is challenging—and sometimes impossible—to identify related entities in a relational database when the exact set of intermediate relationships between those entities is unknown, which is frequently true for complex biomedical data. 7 A newer database technology—known as graph databases—has emerged to solve these and other problems. Instead of using a rigid tabular schema, a graph database consists of nodes (representing individual entities), edges (specific relationships linking a pair of two entities), and attributes (additional data elements attached to either nodes or edges). They emerged out of the computer science concept of a graph, 8 which is analogous to a biological network, and have recently been applied to many domains including IT operations, fraud detection, and machine learning. 9 Graph databases are tremendously flexible and provide efficient and intuitive support for complex queries that reveal higher-order network relationships in multimodal data. Closely related to graph databases is the idea of knowledge graphs—graph representations of consensus knowledge pertaining to a certain domain, summarizing both expert understanding and the combined results of many experimental data points. Knowledge graphs have wide applications in biomedicine, both for delivering existing knowledge and for discovering new knowledge via either aggregating siloed information or generating entirely new inferences (e.g., with machine learning). 10 They are increasingly being used for both of these purposes in disparate areas of biomedicine. For example, the Robokop system 11 (developed by the Renaissance Computer Institute; RENCI) and the Hetionet project 12 deliver general biomedical knowledge powered by semantic knowledge graphs and can be integrated into larger artificial intelligence systems. The OpenPHACTS project also maintains another knowledge graph—called the Open Life Sciences Knowledge Graph 13 —to deliver knowledge describing connections between compounds, targets, diseases, and other entities pertinent to pharmacology and drug discovery/repurposing. In this article, we present ComptoxAI—a new data infrastructure for AI research in computational and predictive toxicology based on a graph-formatted knowledge base and introduce its functionality using three practical use-cases. To the best of our knowledge, ComptoxAI is the first multimodal graph-formatted knowledge base for toxicology data, and one of the first informatics tools designed to support research in toxicology for a diverse range of user types. It is designed for use by diverse stakeholders, including toxicologists, data scientists, regulatory officials, and others. In a previous study, 14 we demonstrated a proof of concept that shows ComptoxAI’s potential to build cutting-edge graph machine learning models for predicting chemical assay responses to specific toxicants, but here we focus on complex information retrieval needs that are relevant to generating new hypotheses in toxicology research. We show that ComptoxAI can easily provide links between chemical exposures, pathways, and system nodes that may explain toxic outcomes, and improve quantitative structure–activity relationship (QSAR) analyses that are infeasible using traditional databases. The knowledge base can generate datasets for predictive modeling that previously required a tremendous amount of manual curation. ComptoxAI is a rapidly growing resource that is freely available to all users, and we strongly encourage community engagement to enhance its development. EXPERIMENTAL PROCEDURES ComptoxAI provides a set of key features that will be expanded upon over the course of its ongoing development. At its core, ComptoxAI contains a large, graph-formatted knowledge base (implemented in Neo4j v4.4.0) that describes entities relevant to computational toxicology and the relationships between those entities. Users can access the knowledge base in several ways, including a browser-based graphical interface, a simplified set of data query tools on the ComptoxAI website, or programmatically by either a REST web API or an accompanying software package written for the Python programming language. Each of these tools can be accessed by visiting ComptoxAI’s homepage at https://comptox.ai/ . The homepage of the website at the time of writing is shown in Figure 1 . Figure 1. Open in a new tab ComptoxAI homepage, showing navbar links to major features (data browsing tools, installation guide, documentation, etc.), a hierarchy diagram of the major entity types included in the knowledge base, and additional introductory text. The website is available at https://comptox.ai . We prototyped the structure of the knowledge base by first building an OWL ontology describing the types of entities relevant to chemical toxicity and the systems-level effects of toxic exposures. These entities include chemicals, genes, biochemical pathways, adverse outcomes, organ systems, assays, and others. The ontology can be thought of as a template based on expert knowledge that maintains consistency between the entities in the knowledge base, their attributes, and the relationships that link those entities. After building the ontology, we assembled a collection of open-access, third-party databases and used them to “fill in” the template provided by the ontology, merging and restructuring data as needed. A list of the external data sources is given in Table 1 . We chose sources that can be considered consensus knowledge rather than raw data, e.g., expert-curated or the summarized results of many individual experiments. Some of these knowledge sources are, themselves, curated interpretations of other raw datasets; Hetionet in particular includes knowledge extracted from a number of source databases including the LINCS L1000 database, the Gene Ontology, Reactome, and others. By structuring the graph database using the ontology, there is a semantically meaningful structure to the database representing real-world biological and chemical knowledge, which is tremendously valuable for improving human understanding of the database’s contents as well as for building interpretable AI and machine learning applications on top of the knowledge base. 15 , 16 Table 1. Publicly Available Source Databases Used in ComptoxAI’s Knowledge Base at the Time of Writing a database name used for: URL PubChem chemicals https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/ DSSTox (US EPA) chemicals, chemical Lists https://comptox.epa.gov/dashboard/ NCBI Gene genes https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/gene DrugBank chemicals https://go.drugbank.com/ AOP-DB adverse outcome pathways https://aopdb.epa.gov/ AOP-Wiki AOP key event relationships https://aopwiki.org/ Tox21 assays, chemical-assay relationships https://tripod.nih.gov/tox21 DisGeNET diseases, disease—gene relationships https://www.disgenet.org/ WikiPathways pathways https://www.wikipathways.org/ Reactome pathways https://reactome.org/ Pathway Interaction Database pathways https://github.com/NCIP/pathway-interaction-database Comparative Toxicogenomics Database chemicals http://ctdbase.org/ Hetionet relationships https://het.io/ Open in a new tab a The list and scope of databases will grow over time as ComptoxAI’s development continues. Additional details—including open-access license terms for all source databases—are available on the ComptoxAI source code repository ( https://github.com/JDRomano2/comptox_ai ). Another important consequence of using ontology as a template for populating the knowledge base is that it allows us to automatically enforce data consistency and data quality standards. For every node type or edge type added to the knowledge base, we define a model that includes relevant node/edge properties formats, where those properties are defined, a source for the display name (and, when applicable, synonyms) of the entity, and a “primary source” that dictates how we define distinct entities. In the case of chemicals, the primary source is the EPA’s DSSTox database, and therefore every chemical in the knowledge base corresponds to exactly one chemical substance in DSSTox. We encode these choices into the ontology, and when we populate the ontology, the choices act as “rules” that trigger an error whenever data inconsistencies are encountered. Furthermore, as mentioned above, we intentionally choose source databases that are known to be manually curated or are accompanied by specific quality control metrics. For example, edges in the knowledge base corresponding to gene–disease associations (originally from the DisGeNet database) are annotated with scores that describe the relative amount of evidence that supports that association. Chemical nodes include a field named “quality control level”, which indicates the specific quality control steps taken by the curators of the DSSTox database. Like other node and edge properties in the graph, these are documented on the ComptoxAI website. If users discover errors, inaccuracies, or other issues in the knowledge base, they can easily submit a templated report on GitHub via the dedicated issue tracking interface. After prototyping the knowledge base structure and populating it with an initial set of source databases, we designed a set of tools and a package of code for the Python programming language that can be used to more conveniently access and query the data in the knowledge base. Finally, we constructed web pages containing documentation of ComptoxAI’s various components, information about computational and predictive toxicology, and a set of user-friendly tools that provide a graphical front-end to some of the most important functions provided by the Python package. The structure of both the code and the database of ComptoxAI was designed to easily allow the incorporation of new data elements (both via updates to already-included source databases and through the inclusion of entirely new source databases). We ensured that all portions of ComptoxAI are open source and open access. All code is licensed under the MIT license, and all data in the knowledge base are available for reuse via Creative Commons licenses. Briefly, the data in the knowledge base are available under the same terms as the most restrictive data reuse license in the set of ComptoxAI’s source databases. None of the source databases are closed access, and their license terms are copied in the ComptoxAI source code repository. Knowledge Base Description. At the time of writing, ComptoxAI’s knowledge base contains 848,475 nodes (entities) and 1,304,899 edges (relationships connecting entities). All entities are a member of at least one of 22 distinct “node types” (e.g., Chemical, Gene, Disease, Assay), and all relationships are a member of one of 30 distinct “relationship types” (e.g., “[Chemical] activates [Assay]” or “[Gene] member of [Pathway]”). The knowledge base is represented as a single large graph that is roughly analogous to a biological network, with the added functionality provided by the ontology and the expanded scope of the entities contained in the graph. The most prevalent node type is “chemical”, comprising 780,037 nodes. A list of the current entity types and counts of those entities is given in Table 2 . Relationship types and their counts are provided in the Supporting Information ( Table S1 ). Table 2. Counts of Entity Types Currently in ComptoxAI’s Knowledge Base entity type n chemical 780,037 gene 62,407 pathway 4570 key event 1111 chemical list 311 adverse outcome pathway 280 molecular initiating event 193 adverse outcome 156 assay 68 Open in a new tab As a rule, we do not collapse multiple related chemical structures (e.g., acids and their salts) into a single chemical record since most leading chemical databases encode them separately and assign distinct regulatory numbers (e.g., CAS RN) to each form. In the case of biological endpoints (e.g., chemical-assay relationships), it can be assumed that experiments were carried out at physiological pH, and therefore the annotations are representative of the p K a of the compound, even if not explicitly represented as such. Furthermore, we do not place any rigid restrictions on the classes of molecules encoded as chemical entities. Instead, we include every chemical present in the DSSTox database, which tend to be small molecules. Most (>93%) of the chemicals have a molecular weight under 500 Da, and the largest chemicals (up to a maximum of 7,594 Da) are primarily biologic drugs. Data Interfaces. ComptoxAI provides various data interfaces (both graphical and programmatic) intended for different classes of users and different data needs. The graphical interfaces include a simplified data query and information retrieval application ( Figure 2 ), a Neo4j graph database browser ( Figure 3 ), a molecular descriptor dataset generator for QSAR modeling (see “ Use-Case 3 ”), and an Adverse Outcome Pathway (AOP) explorer. The data query tool lets users search for entities by various properties (including common name and synonyms) and includes a module that allows searching for chemicals by drawing a query structure. Structure-based search is powered by Marvin JS, version 22.11.0, Chemaxon ( https://www.chemaxon.com ). Text-based search supports case-insensitive and partial matches. Users can also retrieve groups of functionally or structurally related chemicals via “chemical lists,” provided by the EPA. ComptoxAI also provides three nongraphical (i.e., programmatic) interfaces: A Python package that facilitates easy access to the knowledge base and functionalities for extracting and analyzing specific datasets, a REST web API, and direct access to a public copy of the knowledge base via the Cypher query language and Neo4j’s open-source database software. Users have the option of downloading the entire preassembled graph database to be deployed locally. For users interested in customizing the knowledge base (either to extend its scope in the context of toxicology or to adapt it to a different domain), another option is to run the included Python scripts to build the database “from scratch” from local copies of the third-party data source files, a process that is documented on the ComptoxAI website. This also facilitates community contributions to ComptoxAI: Users can prototype changes on their local computer, and if they feel the changes are beneficial to ComptoxAI’s general user base they can then submit these changes as a “pull request” to the main GitHub repository. If the modifications pass community review, they can then be merged into the main codebase permanently. The GitHub repository provides a “CONTRIBUTING.md” document that outlines this process in detail—more information is given in the User Contributions to ComptoxAI section. Video tutorials on how to use ComptoxAI’s major features are available on the website at https://comptox.ai/use/tutorials.html . Figure 2. Open in a new tab Interactive data portal for ComptoxAI, demonstrating how individual nodes (entities) in the knowledge base can be queried. The gene shown is found by searching for the gene symbol CYP2E1, and details about the gene are represented in the “Node details” panel. The identified gene can be sent to other data browsing utilities using the buttons at the bottom of the panel. Out of view are components for displaying relationships and network paths. Figure 3. Open in a new tab ComptoxAI’s Neo4j graph database browser. Users can manually expand and manipulate subgraphs and can also use the powerful Cypher query language to submit database queries or call specific subroutines. In this interface, the left panel allows users to query a random subset of specific node labels (entity types) or relationship types, and the right portion of the network view panel lists the properties annotated to the currently selected node in the network. RESULTS We implemented three use-cases to demonstrate the flexibility and performance of ComptoxAI’s tools when applied to diverse tasks relevant to computational toxicology. Use-cases 1 and 2 both illustrate information retrieval tasks based on existing knowledge, demonstrating the value of assembling heterogeneous data originally fragmented across multiple independent source databases. Use-Case 3 provides an example of making new, previously unobserved predictions via machine learning, showing that ComptoxAI’s data and included tools make it easy to produce datasets for predictive modeling. Although the users have the flexibility to construct their own predictive models and interpret the results, ComptoxAI lowers the barrier presented by inconsistent and poorly structured training data. Each of these use-cases is accompanied by an interactive Jupyter notebook (permanent links are given in the Supporting Information and at the https://comptox.ai/ website) that users can use to reconstruct and reproduce the analyses. Since the contents of the graph database are continually being updated and improved, the exact results returned by any of these use-cases may vary slightly over time, especially when large and particularly complex queries are used. Use-Case 1: Exploring Network Relationships Linking PFOA and Nonalcoholic Fatty Liver Disease. Perfluorooctanoic acid (PFOA) is a surfactant used in many industrial contexts that is strongly associated with serious health outcomes including cancers, 18 ulcerative colitis, 19 thyroid disease, 20 high cholesterol, 21 and others. 22 Although many major chemical manufacturers have phased out use of PFOA and other polyfluoroalkyl substances, they are extremely persistent in the environment and are detectable in the blood of roughly 98% of the U.S. population. In addition to the previously mentioned adverse outcomes, ongoing research is being conducted to understand possible connections between PFOA exposure and nonalcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD). 23 We used ComptoxAI’s “shortest paths” feature to find specific mechanistic links between PFOA and NAFLD. This tool works by finding a collection of the shortest paths in the graph database linking the two nodes of interest, and then joining those paths (which usually overlap) into a small network of paths that separate the two nodes. Roughly, each path from PFOA to NAFLD corresponds to a single mechanistic hypothesis that can be further investigated in a follow-up experiment, either by searching for additional corroborating evidence in other databases or by performing traditional bench experiments to directly observe whether the proposed mechanisms are validated either in vivo or in vitro. A visualization of the shortest paths linking PFOA to NAFLD is shown in Figure 4 . Importantly, the relationships shown in the graph both recapitulate known mechanisms of toxicity-induced NAFLD as well as suggest new genetic mechanisms that have not yet been described. Known mechanisms of toxicity implicate the PPAR- α , PPAR- δ , and ADIPOQ proteins. PPARs regulate the uptake and utilization of fatty acids in many tissues (including liver tissue), 23 and downregulation of the ADIPOQ gene impairs the function of several fatty acid metabolic pathways. 24 New experimental evidence also supports an association between ADH1B and NAFLD severity, 25 and transgenic mouse data show that lower expression of CAT results in a higher likelihood of developing fatty liver and type-2 diabetes mellitus. 26 Furthermore, the visualization includes genes with biologically plausible roles in NAFLD etiology ( SREBF1 is involved in lipogenesis in both adipose and liver tissue, which are in turn associated with NAFLD, 27 and PDK4 regulates insulin action and the metabolism of fatty acids, and plays a role in liver tissue regeneration 28 ). Other genes, including NFE2L2 and CD14 , are to the best of our knowledge entirely novel and can therefore be thought of as new hypotheses generating to explore how PFOA and other toxicants can lead to NAFLD or pathologically similar diseases. Anecdotally, all of the genes implicated in Figure 4 are expressed at high levels in liver tissue, as indicated by gene-level summaries from GeneCards. 29 , 30 Figure 4. Open in a new tab Network diagram of shortest paths joining perfluorooctanoic acid (PFOA) with nonalcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD) in ComptoxAI. The network indicates 14 genes associated with NAFLD, 7 of which are downregulated and 8 of which are upregulated (1 is both) by PFOA. Gene nodes are arranged (via interactive drag-and-drop) to group downregulated (top) and upregulated (bottom) genes. Full-resolution image available on FigShare. 17 Overall, this use-case demonstrates the value of ComptoxAI for discovering associations between toxicological phenomena (in this case, exposure to PFOA and the human disease NAFLD) that are mediated by relationships to other entities. Here, the mediating entities all happen to be genes, but ComptoxAI is flexible in allowing similar questions to be asked of other data types. For example, when we request the shortest paths between the “sonic hedgehog agonist” assay and the “sonic hedgehog receptor ptc1 regulates cell cycle”, shortest paths of the general form [Assay]- > [Chemical]- > [Gene]- > [Pathway] are found. The genes in these paths include PTCH1 , XPO1 , CCNH , CDK1 , RASGRF1 , and others, and each path can be inspected to produce hypotheses for which genes are acted upon by certain toxicants to disrupt cell cycle regulation in a manner mediated by the sonic hedgehog receptor PTCH1. Use-Case 2: Visualizing Network Relationships Involved in Aryl Hydrocarbon Receptor-Mediated Adverse Outcomes of Toxicity. 2,3,7,8-Tetrachlorodibenzo- p -dioxin (known as TCDD or simply dioxin) is a highly toxic organic pollutant that is well known as both a nongenotoxic carcinogen and disruptor of human developmental processes. The Agency for Toxic Substances and Disease Registry (ATSDR) considers dioxin to be one of the substances that poses “the most significant potential threat to human health due to [its] known or suspected toxicity.” 31 The activity of TCDD is primarily mediated by binding to the aryl hydrocarbon receptor (AHR)-a cytosolic transcription factor that regulates the expression of a diverse array of genes that are connected to many biochemical processes. 32 Over several decades, a constellation of associations has been described and characterized between AHR ligands (including dioxin) and adverse health outcomes, including developmental abnormalities, infertility, liver steatosis, immunotoxicity, and various cancers, among others. However, these associations are usually investigated in isolation, focusing on a single mechanism and outcome rather than looking at a more global systems-level view of the impact that AHR ligands have on overall human health. With this in mind, we used ComptoxAI to explore AHR-mediated toxicity using its “expand network” feature. Briefly, this feature allows users to specify one or more entities in the graph (in this case, the node representing dioxin) and then “grow” a network outward from those entities to show their shared associations in a broader context. A visualization of the network diagram generated by ComptoxAI is shown in Figure 5 . The network generated by ComptoxAI shows a distinct pattern characterized by four communities linked by several “hub” nodes, the most obvious of which is dioxin. Key event nodes representing AHR, as expected, also feature prominently see, for example, the orange node immediately above dioxin, labeled “Activation, Long term AHR receptor”. Users can specify parameters indicating the number of levels in the expanded network—higher values return successively larger networks including nodes that are a greater number of steps away from the query node(s). In this case, we used the default parameter value “max level = 4”, which resulted in a network that was sufficiently complex to highlight large-scale structural patterns while not being too large to easily interpret. Figure 5. Open in a new tab Adverse outcome pathway network generated by expanding a network around the node representing dioxin (2,3,7,8-tetrachlorodibenzo- p -dioxin), with network communities labeled in red. Communities roughly correspond to the following adverse outcomes: (A.) liver steatosis, (B.) impaired fertility and bile acid regulation, (C.) long-term risk of liver cancer, and (D.) other liver cancer, especially hepatocellular carcinoma. Dioxin (2,3,7,8-tetrachlorodibenzo- p -dioxin) plays a central role as a “hub” in the network. Node types are chemicals (red), AOPs (green), molecular initiating events (orange), and key events (purple). Full-resolution image available on Figshare. 33 The four communities roughly correspond to patterns of disease phenotypes: (A) Liver steatosis, (B) impaired fertility and bile acid regulation, (C) long-term risk of liver cancer, and (D) other liver cancers. There are many potential interpretations for the meanings of these communities, but one natural interpretation is that they indicate shared mechanisms underlying the molecular etiology of those diseases. Of note are the colocation of reproductive adverse outcomes in community A and infertility in community B. Although phenotypically related, the AOP network suggests different underlying mechanisms: While reproductive dysfunction and developmental abnormalities seem to be mediated by gonadotropin-releasing hormone receptor (GnRHR), infertility instead seems to be mediated by activation of PPAR- α . However, as both are components of complex systems, there may be a hidden association that is simply not present in the AOP source data used to populate ComptoxAI (e.g., an interaction between GnRHR and PPAR- α ). Users familiar with AHR may note what seem to be omissions in the network at first glance. For example, AHR is a member of a larger group of transcription factors—named the basic-helix/loop/helix per-Arnt-sim family—that is implicated in diverse functions such as regulating Circadian rhythm, 34 but relevant key events and adverse outcomes are not present in Figure 5 . However, if the radius of the network is expanded (e.g., to “max level = 5”), these relevant entities are then included. Briefly, to visualize networks in ComptoxAI effectively, it is often necessary to limit the size of the network at the expense of omitting potentially useful information. For this reason, users have the option to retrieve networks as JSON-formatted data structures rather than interactive network diagrams so that larger, more complex networks can be processed programmatically rather than visually. Beyond describing potentially coregulated communities of adverse outcomes, another interesting characteristic of the network is the nodes that “bridge” those communities. As mentioned above, dioxin plays the most prominent role in bridging the communities in the network, with direct connections to key events in three of the four communities. Anecdotally, this validates the potential utility of a knowledge graph-based approach used by ComptoxAI: We initially queried dioxin with the knowledge that it is an important toxicant that targets AHR, and the knowledge representation returned by ComptoxAI directly reflects that key role. Another intriguing example is given by the nodes for NFE2L2 / FXR and NR1H4 , which bridge communities A (liver steatosis) and B (fertility and bile acid). Although not coded for in ComptoxAI, a significant body of work has shown NFE2L2 / FXR and NR1H4 —in addition to regulating bile acid synthesis and transport—is also involved in the innate immune response. 35 – 37 Although a link between dioxin exposure and AHR-mediated immune system dysfunction has been proposed for well over a decade, 38 a precise mechanism has remained elusive. Given the key structural role these nodes play in the AHR/dioxin AOP network, this provides a tempting hypothesis that can be explored in future work that dioxin interactions with AHR can trigger downstream effects that modulate the activity of NFE2L2 / RXR and NR1H4 , which in turn are linked to a variety of disease phenotypes that involve both bile acid synthesis and immune dysfunction. It should be noted, though, that ComptoxAI does not currently report a link between these genes and immune function, but rather that we had to use external expert knowledge. Later, we discuss planned future additions to ComptoxAI that will allow robust prediction of associations that are not directly coded for in our source databases, specifically via graph machine learning and ontology reasoning. Nonetheless, the “hub node” placement of these genes of interest allows users to quickly identify that they may play crucial regulatory roles in several AOPs that lead to multiple adverse outcomes. Use-Case 3: QSAR Dataset Generation-Predicting PXR Agonism in Pesticide Ingredients. Quantitative structure–activity relationship (QSAR) modeling is one of the most common techniques in predictive toxicology. Briefly, QSAR consists of building a predictive model of the form y ^ = f ( x ) + ε where x is a dataset of molecular descriptors of chemical structure, y ^ is the estimated measure of activity (e.g., a specific toxic mode of action), and ε is a term that captures the error of the predictive model. 39 f (•) is any suitable predictive model; common choices include logistic regression, random forest, and gradient boosting classifiers. Usually, the model is first trained by fitting the model to a set of training data where the outcome of interest is known, and then evaluated on a set of testing data where the outcome is unknown to the predictive model. ComptoxAI provides both graphical and programmatic interfaces for generating QSAR datasets. An image of the graphical interface for this tool is shown in Figure 6 . Figure 6. Open in a new tab Screenshot image of the “Build QSAR Dataset” module from the ComptoxAI website. Users specify an assay endpoint, (optional) list of related chemicals to filter by, whether to include a “discovery” dataset of chemicals with no known assay measurements for making novel predictions, and format of the dataset. The dataset is generated dynamically and returned within several seconds of submitting the query. To test this feature, we had ComptoxAI generate a QSAR dataset consisting of chemicals in the US EPA’s list of 4021 chemicals classified as pesticidal “active ingredients.” Pesticides are of particular interest to predictive toxicology because they usually exploit a mechanism of toxicity in a target pest organism, but they may or may not similarly cause toxic outcomes in humans. In this case, we specify the predictive target of our QSAR dataset to be agonism of the human pregnane X receptor (PXR), which is a nuclear receptor that senses a broad range of foreign toxic substances, activating the CYP3A4 gene and subsequently triggering a variety of downstream responses that metabolize xenobiotic chemicals. 40 PXR agonism is one of 68 assays sourced from the Tox21 toxicity screening database 41 – 43 that are represented in ComptoxAI, and one that we highlighted in a previous study 14 demonstrating that ComptoxAI’s graph machine learning models significantly outperform traditional QSAR models. However, traditional QSAR (e.g., using standard, nongraph machine learning) is still the simplest and most familiar approach to most researchers and will likely perform better than graph machine learning in scenarios where the chemicals of interest are poorly studied and lack annotations to other entities in ComptoxAI’s knowledge base. By setting PXR agonism as the predictive target, ComptoxAI generates a tabular dataset consisting of samples that correspond to individual pesticide chemicals and predictive features (variables) that correspond to the individual bits of the MACCS chemical structure fingerprint (Molecular ACCess System —a 166-bit structural key developed by MDL Information Systems that is widely used by open-source cheminformatics software packages and provides good coverage of structural characteristics that are relevant to biological activity). 44 By default, ComptoxAI returns a target vector, where in this case, “1” indicates a chemical does cause PXR agonism based on the Tox21 data and “0” indicates that the chemical does not cause PXR agonism. We omit edges between chemicals and assays in ComptoxAI when the Tox21 data deem a result “inconclusive,” but these edges can potentially be recovered in the QSAR results. The example we focus on below uses these binary prediction targets, as they are the most easily interpretable values and allow the trained QSAR model to be evaluated using the most straightforward performance metrics. Alternatively, users can specify continuous endpoints (such as IC 50 /EC 50 ), which are stored as values annotated to the same edges used to derive the binary labels used in this use-case. To serve as a proof of concept, we trained a simple random forest classifier—a well-established model frequently used inQSAR 45 —on the tabular dataset and evaluated the performance of the model on a random holdout sample of the data not used during training. Specific details for our implementation of the classifier are provided in the Supporting Information , along with an interactive version of our code that will allow users to reproduce the analysis. Note that users can easily modify the analysis to fit their own needs—although we generated a QSAR dataset designed to predict PXR toxicity from pesticide active ingredients using a random forest classifier, any of these are easily modified, and any external data analytics software can be used to fit a predictive model. Also of note, the entire process of generating the dataset—fully annotated and ready to be used in common machine learning and data analytics software—is accomplished in a single command issued to ComptoxAI. Although Figure 6 shows the graphical interface for the purpose of demonstration, this task can be accomplished just as easily within a programmatic environment, allowing users to construct many QSAR datasets and predictive models in an automated fashion. The random forest classifier predicts PXR agonism with 86.6% accuracy (precision = 0.85, recall = 0.73), which is generally quite high for a QSAR analysis. We attribute this performance to the relatively large dataset available in ComptoxAI, the power of using MACCS to capture structural characteristics of chemicals, and the quality of chemical assay annotations in ComptoxAI. After establishing the model’s performance, we applied the trained model to 1,092 pesticide chemicals in ComptoxAI with no experimental evidence for PXR agonism (in other words, with no edges between the chemical’s node and the “PXR agonism” assay node), which we refer to as a “discovery dataset.” The predictive model yields probability scores indicating the likelihood of these new chemicals causing PXR agonism. A sample of the 10 chemicals with the highest probability of PXR agonism is shown in Table 3 . Users who are interested in reusing the trained QSAR model can download the source code from FigShare 46 (for additional model details, see the Supplemental Information ). Table 3. PXR Agonism Predictions for Chemicals in the PXR Assay “Discovery Dataset” a chemical Pr(PXR agonism) tetrachlorophene >0.99 ζ -cypermethrin 0.98 d -trans- β -cypermethrin 0.98 2-chloro-6-phenylphenol 0.96 3,5-dichloro-2-biphenylol 0.96 desmetryn 0.96 cyanofenphos 0.95 azaconazole 0.95 gardona (trans-isomer) 0.95 phenmedipham-ethyl 0.94 Open in a new tab a Only the 10 chemicals (of 1,092 total) with the highest probability are shown. This use-case demonstrates ComptoxAI’s ability to rapidly generate high-quality tabular datasets that can be used to answer specific toxicological questions. The included tool for doing so is highly flexible and allows for both graphical and programmatic specification of dataset parameters. Users can analyze the datasets in any external data analysis or machine learning environment that they prefer. Future additions to ComptoxAI will include more specialized graph machine learning predictive models that we anticipate will continue to outperform current state-of-the-art models. DISCUSSION Graph-Formatted Knowledge Bases vs Traditional Databases for Discovery in Toxicology. A prevailing concept in informatics is that useful knowledge is often fragmented across multiple unlinked sources and that this fragmentation is a major barrier to discovering important associations in the natural world, such as those linking chemical exposures to specific clinical outcomes (both harmful and therapeutic). A classic example of this phenomenon is given by Swanson in 1986: Unrelated collections of scientific studies described that (a) Raynaud’s syndrome is a disease caused by poor circulation and (b) dietary fish oil could significantly improve circulation, but no literature at the time suggested that fish oil could be an effective therapy for Raynaud’s syndrome. 47 In the following years, it was shown that this therapeutic link does exist, and fish oil is an effective treatment for primary Raynaud’s syndrome. Although this was described as early as 1986, this issue persists to this day due to fragmentation both between data sources (e.g., siloed databases with no direct means for interoperability) and within data sources (e.g., relational databases separating data of different modalities into distinct tables). The graph database approach of ComptoxAI to unify data from numerous public data sources is a targeted approach that solves both of these sources of knowledge fragmentation. This is a consequence of graph databases representing all entities in a single, large network that is explicitly designed to infer connections between those entities even when they are not directly linked. The three use-cases we have provided illustrate complex but natural questions relating to timely research problems in toxicology, and in each case, we were able to either solve a data need or propose multiple semantically meaningful hypotheses in a single operation. We have also demonstrated that effective use of the knowledge base does not require a deep understanding of data science or informatics, and that through careful preparation of user-friendly tools and adequate documentation many types of users can be enabled to use ComptoxAI. Much of ComptoxAI’s expressiveness and flexibility is a direct consequence of its knowledge base’s highly heterogeneous nature. Most scientific databases focus on one or a few types of entities, but ComptoxAI can be expanded to include anything deemed relevant for computational toxicology without any major changes to the underlying architecture. Another consequence of ComptoxAI’s design is shown in Table 1 : As one would expect for a toxicology knowledge base, the most prevalent entity type is Chemical, representing nearly 92% ( n = 780,037) of all nodes in the knowledge base. On its own, the task of curating such a massive number of chemicals in the context of a single analysis is tremendously daunting, but many of the other entity types in ComptoxAI’s knowledge base (especially chemical lists, adverse outcome pathways, and metabolic pathways) provide highly convenient functionality for extracting from the total population of chemicals smaller subsets with particular functional, structural, or biological characteristics, which is usually true for most applied predictive toxicology tasks. Another useful characteristic of the knowledge base is that the majority of the chemicals (657,048; roughly 84%) are “orphan” nodes—they have no direct entities to other entities in the knowledge base. Although this might initially seem like a deficiency, it is a result of most chemicals of possible toxicological concern being virtually unstudied, and the predictive models included with/enabled by ComptoxAI allow users to predict where links between orphan chemical nodes and other entities in the knowledge graph should or could exist. Data Quality Considerations. Any database or knowledge base intended for secondary use needs to consider the consistency and quality of the data contained therein. Since ComptoxAI’s knowledge base is composed of information integrated from other, third-party data sources, we devote a considerable amount of effort to ensure the data are not substantially altered while assembling the knowledge base. This means that any inaccuracies or errors present in the original data sources will likely propagate to ComptoxAI, as well. However, we have two mechanisms for mitigating the effect this has on the quality of the knowledge base: (1) Where appropriate and available, we provide measures of confidence in the entity representations and relationship annotation quality, such as the quality control scores on chemical nodes or the annotation scores on chemical-disease edges and (2) we maintain data provenance by including cross-references to many third-party data sources (especially the ones used directly to build the knowledge base) and by publishing all of the code used to integrate the source data into the intermediary ontology format. Similarly, we provide various tools for making new inferences and predictions on the knowledge base without explicitly incorporating the results of those predictions into the knowledge base itself. This is mainly because doing so would lead to the knowledge base including information that is not accepted as fact by the toxicological research community at large, thus violating our crucial claim that ComptoxAI is a knowledge base rather than a database. Current Limitations. An issue shared by all knowledge bases is that they are—as implied by the name—limited by the extent of current knowledge. This holds true for ComptoxAI: Although we have demonstrated the broad potential for making new discoveries via ComptoxAI, those discoveries must be derived from one or more known associations between entities in the knowledge base, and those associations need to be represented in at least one of the source databases used to populate ComptoxAI. Discoveries made with ComptoxAI generally follow one of two paradigms: First, by inferring relations that require multiple “hops” across the knowledge graph, chaining together several already known facts that were previously fragmented across several disconnected source databases (e.g., which is illustrated in Use-Cases 1 and 2). Second, using machine learning or other quantitative approaches to predict new relationships in the knowledge base by exploiting and analyzing patterns in the knowledge base’s network structure (e.g., which is illustrated in Use-Case 3 ). The first paradigm is already well supported and is consistent with much of the information retrieval and information synthesis functionality that we try to emphasize in this paper. The second paradigm is partially supported, as we have demonstrated by generating a QSAR dataset that is then fit to a random forest prediction model. Our next primary area of focus—which is expanded upon in the following section—involves the creation of graph machine learning models and semantic data inference tools that will provide better “out-of-the-box” support for predicting phenomena that have not yet been observed. Future Plans for ComptoxAI. As mentioned above, ComptoxAI is under active and ongoing development, and this paper is meant to highlight its first round of features meant primarily to support information retrieval and data access within ComptoxAI’s graph knowledge base. We are continually evaluating new public data sources to add to the knowledge base and monitor for incremental updates to data sources that are already being used. Soon, we plan to add genetic variants and proteins to the knowledge base (currently, only genes are included, with the implicit assumption that each gene may code for a variable number of proteins or protein isoforms). Since the individual contributions of a particular genetic variant or protein isoform/isozyme are incredibly context-dependent, we are evaluating both source databases for these entities as well as the feasibility of representing different aspects of their regulatory contexts, including cell-type specificity, relative levels of expression, roles in development and changes over time, epistasis, and others. Additionally, we are considering techniques for representing disagreement (e.g., when two databases list different variants for the same gene) and lack of consensus (e.g., when only weak evidence links a genetic variant to a phenotype). Mechanisms of toxicity also can be highly specific to cell lines or tissue types, and we plan to incorporate cell types and their relative gene/protein expression levels into the knowledge base. Beyond new additions to the knowledge base itself, we will continue to evaluate and develop new tools to improve usability (similar to the graphical data browser, QSAR generation tool, and adverse outcome pathway explorer described above), particularly for users who are not comfortable writing manual database queries or working with the programmatic interface to ComptoxAI. These include a question-answering feature where users will be able to ask questions of the knowledge base in the natural text (e.g., “Which metabolic pathways are most dysregulated by polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons?”) and receive structured answers as a response (e.g., a list of pathway names along with scores that represent levels of evidence supporting the association). Finally—and as briefly described above—we plan to include a variety of informatics and machine learning features to support cutting-edge AI models (particularly ones that outperform “traditional” predictive models like QSAR and read-across) and data interoperability. We are especially interested in releasing a gallery of graph machine learning and deep neural network models that use the graph structure of the knowledge base to greatly outperform traditional machine learning analyses (see Romano et al. 14 for an initial proof of concept demonstrating this approach). For data interoperability, ComptoxAI’s long-term plan includes the development of an OpenAPI-compatible specification that is compatible with the NCATS Biomedical Data Translator project 48 and includes modules to serialize data to the Fast Healthcare Interoperability Resources standard, 49 both of which will allow ComptoxAI to function in a larger ecosystem of intelligent, interoperable systems that combine evidence from diverse domains to solve complex problems in understanding human and environmental health. In an upcoming publication, we will further detail the informatics infrastructure underlying ComptoxAI, the process used to construct the knowledge base, and how these were performed to maximize the generalizability of the technology to domains other than toxicology. These and other new developments can be monitored regularly on the https://comptox.ai/ website, as well as the source code repository (see “Code availability”, below). User Contributions to ComptoxAI. Users can help contribute to the development of ComptoxAI in several ways. The ComptoxAI website includes a “Contact” page where users can: request new features and/or data types, report software bugs or data inaccuracies, obtain technical assistance for the knowledge base or the ComptoxAI software, and discuss opportunities to reuse or repurpose ComptoxAI for different applications. We also request that users (voluntarily) let us know when they use ComptoxAI in a publication of their own or when they make a new discovery using ComptoxAI’s knowledge base or tools. Users can also indicate whether they would like their work utilizing ComptoxAI to be “show-cased” on the website, which is helpful both for promotion of that research and for allowing us to better understand how ComptoxAI is being used by the broader biomedical community. Software developers also can contribute to ComptoxAI’s codebase by submitting pull-requests to ComptoxAI’s public source code repository on Github ( https://github.com/JDRomano2/comptox_ai ), by viewing the text document titled “CONTRIBUTING.md.” CONCLUSIONS Computational and predictive toxicology are rapidly growing disciplines within pharmacology and environmental health science, but major advancements in methods, tools, and data infrastructure are necessary before researchers can effectively address the most important questions in the field. ComptoxAI aims to tackle each of these simultaneously by providing semantic data resources and user-friendly interfaces that target multiple classes of users. In this paper, we introduce ComptoxAI and demonstrate its information retrieval and knowledge discovery capabilities, illustrated via three different use-cases that focus on real-world questions relevant to modern toxicology research and are designed to be usable with little to no informatics or data science expertise. Along with previous and upcoming studies that address ComptoxAI’s capabilities for performing cutting-edge machine learning analyses and interoperability across disparate third-party data repositories, we are in the process of showcasing its myriad of potential applications. We strongly encourage toxicologists and informaticians to become involved in the development, evaluation, and promotion of ComptoxAI, which is and will continue to be a free, public, and open-source toolkit that can be used for a wide variety of tasks in computational toxicology. Supplementary Material Supplementary Material for "Automating Predictive Toxicology Using ComptoxAI" NIHMS1856636-supplement-Supplementary_Material_for__Automating_Predictive_Toxicology_Using_ComptoxAI_.pdf (71.8KB, pdf) ACKNOWLEDGMENTS The authors thank the authors and maintainers of the third-party data sources aggregated in ComptoxAI’s knowledge base, and especially Dr. Holly Mortensen for her assistance with integrating data from the Adverse Outcome Pathways Database (AOP-DB) into our knowledge graph. Funding This work was made possible through funding by National Institutes of Health grants K99-LM013646 (Romano), T32-ES019851 (Penning, Romano), P30-ES013508 (Penning), and R01-AG066833 (Moore). Footnotes Supporting Information The Supporting Information is available free of charge at https://pubs.acs.org/doi/10.1021/acs.chemrestox.2c00074 . Additional details on the relationship types in the knowledge base as well as a complete description of the random forest QSAR model in Use-Case 3 ( PDF ) Complete contact information is available at: https://pubs.acs.org/10.1021/acs.chemrestox.2c00074 The authors declare the following competing financial interest(s): The authors declare no competing financial interests except T.M.P. who is a member of the Expert Panel, Research Institute for Fragrance Materials. ComptoxAI is open source and freely available to all users under the GNU GPLv3 License. Source code is available on GitHub at https://github.com/JDRomano2/comptox_ai . Data in ComptoxAI’s graph knowledge base are derived from strictly public original sources, which are described online at https://comptox.ai/use/databases.html . All code at the time of writing is available in an archived copy via Zenodo. 50 Contributor Information Joseph D. Romano, Institute for Biomedical Informatics, University of Pennsylvania, Philadelphia, Pennsylvania 19104, United States; Center of Excellence in Environmental Toxicology, University of Pennsylvania, Philadelphia, Pennsylvania 19104, United States Yun Hao, Institute for Biomedical Informatics, University of Pennsylvania, Philadelphia, Pennsylvania 19104, United States. Jason H. Moore, Department of Computational Biomedicine, Cedars-Sinai Medical Center, Los Angeles, California 90069, United States Trevor M. Penning, Center of Excellence in Environmental Toxicology and Department of Systems Pharmacology & Translational Therapeutics, University of Pennsylvania, Philadelphia, Pennsylvania 19104, United States REFERENCES (1). Thomas RS; Bahadori T; Buckley TJ; Cowden J; Deisenroth C; Dionisio KL; Frithsen JB; Grulke CM; Gwinn MR; Harrill JA; Higuchi M; Houck KA; Hughes MF; Hunter ES; Isaacs KK; Judson RS; Knudsen TB; Lambert JC; Linnenbrink M; Martin TM; Newton SR; Padilla S; Patlewicz G; Paul-Friedman K; Phillips KA; Richard AM; Sams R; Shafer TJ; Setzer RW; Shah I; Simmons JE; Simmons SO; Singh A; Sobus JR; Strynar M; Swank A; Tornero-Valez R; Ulrich EM; Villeneuve DL; Wambaugh JF; Wetmore BA; Williams AJ The Next Generation Blueprint of Computational Toxicology at the U.S. Environmental Protection Agency. Toxicol. Sci 2019, 169, 317–332. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] (2). Luechtefeld T; Rowlands C; Hartung T Big-Data and Machine Learning to Revamp Computational Toxicology and Its Use in Risk Assessment. Toxicol. Res 2018, 7, 732–744. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] (3). Atzeni P; Antonellis VD Relational Database Theory; Benjamin/Cummings Publishing Company, 1993. [ Google Scholar ] (4). Mortensen HM; Senn J; Levey T; Langley P; Willi