oBiondo-Wood G, Haber J, editors. Nursing research: methods and critical appraisal for Evidence-Based practice. 8th ed. St. Louis: Elsevier Mosby; 2014. p.182–98. 32. Fraenkel JR, Wallen NE, Hyun HH. How to design and evaluate research in education. Boston: McGraw Hill; 2012. [ Google Scholar ] 33. Hennink M. Focus group discussions: Understanding qualitative research. New York: Oxford University Press; 2014. [ Google Scholar ] 34. Ary D, Jacobs LC, Sorensen C. Introduction to research in education. 8th ed. Belmont, CA, USA: Wadsworth, Cengage Learning;; 2010. [ Google Scholar ] 35. Creswell JW, Miller DL. Determining validity in qualitative inquiry. Theory Into Pract. 2000;39:124–30. [ Google Scholar ] 36. Shenton AK. Strategies for ensuring trustworthiness in qualitative research projects. Educ Inf. 2004;22:63–75. [ Google Scholar ] 37. Merriam SB, Tisdell EJ. Qualitative research: A guide to design and implementation. 4th ed. San Francisco, CA: Jossey-Bass; 2016. [ Google Scholar ] 38. Lincoln YS, Guba EG. Naturalistic inquiry. CA: SAGE Publications, Inc.; 1985. [ Google Scholar ] 39. Creswell JW. Qualitative inquiry and research design: choosing among five approaches. 3rd ed. Thousand Oaks, CA: SAGE Publications, Inc.; 2013. [ Google Scholar ] 40. Marshall C, Rossman GB. Designing qualitative research. 6th ed. Thousand Oaks, CA: SAGE Publications, Inc.; 2016. [ Google Scholar ] 41. Mayring P. Qualitative content analysis: theoretical background and procedures. In: Bikner-Ahsbahs A, Knipping C, Presmeg N, editors. Approaches to qualitative research in mathematics education: examples of methodology and methods. New York; London; Dordrecht; Heidelberg: Springer; 2015. pp. 365–80. [ Google Scholar ] 42. Creswell JW. Educational research: planning, conducting, and evaluating quantitative and qualitative research. Boston: Pearson Education, Inc.; 2012. [ Google Scholar ] 43. Creswell JW. Qualitative inquiry & research design: choosing among five approaches. USA: Sage 