s recommended for betterment of the same. J Evol Med Dent Sci. 2014;3:14923–9. [ Google Scholar ] 53. Amberkar M, Lalit M, Kumari M, Bairy KL. A survey on methods of undergraduate pharmacology teaching. J Pharm Res Health Care. 2011;3:43–9. [ Google Scholar ] 54. Gupta D, Smith MM, Tromp K. Utilization of active learning approaches in medicinal chemistry and subsequent correlations with North American licensure examination and pharmacy curriculum outcomes assessment scores. Curr Pharm Teach Learn. 2021;13:376–81. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 55. Miles S, Kellett J, Leinster SJ. Medical graduates’ preparedness to practice: a comparison of undergraduate medical school training. BMC Med Educ. 2017;17:33. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 56. Shankar PR, Dubey AK, Upadhyay DK, Subish P, Alwar MC. Educational sessions on assessing rationality of prescriptions: student feedback. Pharm Educ. 2006;6:191–5. [ Google Scholar ] 57. Rakanovic-Todic M, Mulabegović N, Kusturica J, Burnazovic L, Loga-Zec S, Cesic AK, et al. Role play in the rational pharmacotherapy training: is it suitable? Technics Technol Educ Manage. 2011;6:535–40. [ Google Scholar ] 58. Byers T, Imms W, Hartnell-Young E. Comparative analysis of the impact of traditional versus innovative learning environment on student attitudes and learning outcomes. Stud Educational Evaluation. 2018;58:167–77. [ Google Scholar ] 59. Budakoğlu Iİ, Coşkun Ö, Kıyak YS, Uluoğlu C. Teaching rational prescribing in undergraduate medical education: a systematic search and review. Eur J Clin Pharmacol. 2023;79:341–8. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 60. Stevens R. Role-play and student engagement: reflections from the classroom. Teach High Educ. 2015;20:481–92. [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Supplementary Materials Supplementary Material 1. (13.7KB, docx) Supplementar